// The SOVEREIGN engine's recursion obligation. Until 2026-09-17 `vra_recursion_terminates`
// existed in vra.h with exactly ONE caller — analysis/effects.h, which used it to decide
// DIVERGE — so it never produced a user-facing verdict, E011/E082 for recursion still came
// from src/sema/, and the project plan recorded twice that no recursion analysis existed.
// An analysis nobody can observe is indistinguishable from one that is not there.
//
// It now raises an obligation (a VRA_TERMINATION check with `recursion` set), reported as E011
// by analysis/report.h with the SELF-CALL's position — which the legacy diagnostic never had.
// The three shapes below are what it must prove; recursion_ir_engine_fail.ln is what it must
// refuse. Together they are the pair that has to exist before a permissive change lands.
extern proc libc_printf(fmt *u8, ...) i32

// 1. a parameter that strictly descends
func countdown(n usize) usize {
    if n <= 1 { return 1 }
    return countdown(n - 1)
}

// 2. a HALVING descent — the octagon's own division fact, not a syntactic pattern
func depth(n usize) usize {
    if n < 1 { return 0 }
    return depth(n / 2) +% 1
}

// 3. a DIFFERENCE of two parameters, which is what divide-and-conquer descends on: neither
//    bound falls on its own, `hi - lo` does. The descent collapses to two variables because
//    one argument is carried through unchanged, and the `lo < hi` guard grounds it.
//
//    Written with the recursion on ONE side, deliberately. The first draft guarded the two
//    calls with `if mid < hi { ... } else { ... }`, which reads as exhaustive and is not
//    provable: on the else path the engine is told `mid >= hi`, so `span(lo, mid)` GROWS the
//    measure there. That the branch is unreachable (mid < hi always holds under `lo < hi`) is
//    a fact about the guard the engine does not get to assume when the guard says otherwise.
//    The refusal was correct and the test was wrong — worth recording, because a test that
//    asserts an engine can prove something it should not is how a false proof gets demanded.
func span(lo usize, hi usize) usize {
    if lo < hi {
        var mid usize = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
        return span(lo, mid)
    }
    return 0
}

proc main() i32 {
    libc_printf("%d %d %d\n", countdown(5) as% i32, depth(16) as% i32, span(0, 8) as% i32)
    return 0
}
